# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .env.example — the shape of .env, with no secrets in it. TRACKED; .env is not.
# Roadmap §50 slice 1. Copy to .env and fill the blanks.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# 32+ chars. Generate a fresh one per environment:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Never reuse the local value in production.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# This app's own origin. Locally the Vite dev server; in production the CANONICAL
# host (DEPLOYMENT §16-K — not chosen yet). The apex-vs-www decision is made ONCE
# and the other host is 308'd at Vercel's EDGE, never in app code (§18.4).
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:5173

# ─── Neon ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Create a FRESH project (the legacy one is deleted separately — DEPLOYMENT §18.7).
# Free tier: 100 CU-hours/project/month, scale-to-zero after 5 min, auto-resume.
#
# USE THE POOLED STRING. Neon shows two; the pooled host contains "-pooler":
#   postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/DB?sslmode=require
#
# This is not a preference. A serverless function opening a DIRECT connection per
# cold invocation exhausts the connection limit under real traffic and keeps the
# compute awake, defeating the scale-to-zero economics Neon was chosen for. It
# works fine on localhost either way and only fails in production, which is the
# class of bug DEPLOYMENT §1 exists to find early.
DATABASE_URL=

# ─── Google OAuth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services → Credentials
#   → Create Credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application
#
# Authorised redirect URI, EXACTLY (Google matches exact strings):
#   http://localhost:5173/api/auth/callback/google
#
# Google permits http://localhost for development, which is why slice 1 needs no
# domain and no deployment. When a production domain exists, add its callback for
# the CANONICAL host ONLY — two registrations drift apart permanently (§18.4).
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ─── Microsoft OAuth (Azure Entra ID) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Full walkthrough in docs/AUTH_SETUP.md §6. Two gotchas that cost the most time:
#
#   1. Supported account types MUST be "Accounts in any organizational directory
#      AND personal Microsoft accounts" — this is what pairs with tenantId
#      'common' in auth.ts. A narrower choice silently excludes the outlook.com
#      and hotmail.com users this provider exists to reach.
#
#   2. On the secret screen copy the VALUE, not the Secret ID. They sit next to
#      each other and the Value is shown once only.
#
# THE SECRET EXPIRES — 24 months maximum, where Google's never does. When it
# lapses, Microsoft sign-in breaks with no deploy and no warning. Set a calendar
# reminder for ~22 months the day you create it.
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Shows the Microsoft button. Set to 1 in the SAME step as the two values above —
# the server registers the provider from the credentials themselves, this only
# controls the button, and they must not drift. Also the kill switch: on the day
# the secret expires, setting this to 0 hides the button without a code change.
PUBLIC_AUTH_MICROSOFT=
